WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.160
In Hollywood, actors and writers usually have
to grind away for years, sometimes even decades,

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.700
before their break comes along.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.940
A big part of that struggle is pursuing the
perfection of the craft - honing their skills

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.990
to the point where their talents can't be
ignored.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.260
It can be an all-consuming process and leave
little time for pursuing any other dreams.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.359
This makes it all the more impressive when
a Hollywood star known primarily for acting

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:24.050
sneaks behind the keyboard and works on a
script.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.449
Here are some of the Hollywood stars who secretly
helped write your favorite movies.

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:29.960
Carrie Fisher

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.190
As far as career-defining roles go, you could
do a lot worse than Princess Leia, one of

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.550
the great onscreen heroines of all time.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.710
Fortunately for us, the late, great Carrie
Fisher wasn't keen to settle for just being

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.129
iconic on screen.

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:47.039
According to Slash Film, she also made a living
as a screenwriter and as one of the most in-demand

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:48.840
script doctors in Hollywood.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.190
Fisher was the primary screenwriter for the
semi-autobiographical film Postcards From

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:57.649
the Edge, as well as These Old Broads, and
she helped fix a variety of scripts over the

00:00:57.649 --> 00:01:02.699
course of her career - her fingerprints are
on everything from Hook to Sister Act to Mr.

00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:03.699
and Mrs. Smith.

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:07.580
She's also on record as saying she rewrote
some of her dialogue as Leia on Return of

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.751
the Jedi, and even helped out with the script
for what ended up being her final film, Star

00:01:11.751 --> 00:01:14.450
Wars Episode 8: The Last Jedi.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.110
Joel Edgerton

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:20.690
Over the last several years, Joel Edgerton
has proven himself a reliable actor, performing

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:23.890
very well in films like Warrior and Zero Dark
Thirty.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:28.460
His filmography is peppered with a writing
credit here and there, but in 2015, he made

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:33.100
his proper debut with a riveting thriller
called The Gift, which he wrote, directed,

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.100
and starred in.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:38.040
The script draws heavily from the structure
and pacing pioneered by acclaimed directors

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:43.400
like Alfred Hitchcock and Brian DePalma, and
critics praised both his performance and the

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.000
work he did offscreen as well.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.150
Edgerton will be back as a writer and director
with the film Boy Erased - and it's looking

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.680
like another career highlight.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:52.680
Wentworth Miller

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:58.470
In 2005, Wentworth Miller hit TV screens everywhere
on the TV show Prison Break.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.950
He proved to be a charismatic, intense performer
and later went on to bring life to comic book

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:07.240
supervillain Captain Cold on the CW's The
Flash and Legends of Tomorrow.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.110
Despite how talented he is onscreen, his work
is considerably mainstream.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.110
His writing?

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.310
Not so much.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:18.230
Miller made his screenwriting debut with Stoker,
a tense, erotic thriller that takes a sadistic

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.450
glee in winding its characters through a downward
spiral.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:26.310
The screenplay is incredibly effective, but
unfortunately, his follow-up, The Disappointments

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.650
Room, wasn't well-received at all.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.410
Here's hoping Stoker wasn't a fluke.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.940
Justin Theroux

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.320
With movie star looks and an easygoing charisma,
Justin Theroux is the kind of actor who could

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.050
easily coast by with a career in mainstream
action movies and romantic comedies.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.590
But his career has been anything but conventional.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:48.460
Theroux is well known for his appearances
in films like Mulholland Drive and for his

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.970
leading role in the HBO series The Leftovers.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.440
The only thing more surprising than Theroux's
choices as an actor is the path he's taken

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.000
as an accomplished writer.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.200
His two most notable scripts are 2008's acclaimed
comedy Tropic Thunder and Marvel's Iron Man

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.990
2, and he also worked on Rock of Ages and
Zoolander 2.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:10.110
Theroux tends to apply his acting talents
to more artistic fare while utilizing his

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:15.880
knack for blockbuster storytelling as a screenwriter
- which seems like a pretty cool career choice.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:17.630
Paul Rudd

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:21.770
It's tough to point to a leading man in modern
comedy more reliable than Paul Rudd.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:26.170
From The 40-Year-Old Virgin to Ant-Man, his
easygoing charm and sense of humor have made

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:27.880
for a great career in the genre.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.330
It only makes sense that he'd have a knack
for it behind the keyboard as well.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.600
Rudd has writing credits on two of his films.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.660
The first is Role Models, a 2008 comedy directed
by Rudd's longtime friend and collaborator

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:39.790
David Wain.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.579
The second featured his biggest role yet,
in 2015's Ant-Man.

00:03:43.579 --> 00:03:47.840
According to Deadline, after the film was
handed off from attached director Edgar Wright,

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.880
Rudd did a rewrite of the script with Anchorman
writer and director Adam McKay.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:56.720
It seems like Rudd may be most memorable onscreen
when he's written the parts he's playing.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:57.720
Jim Rash

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.930
In addition to guest-starring on a wide array
of TV comedies, Jim Rash was also a series

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:05.620
regular on the acclaimed cult classic Community.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.380
With credits like that, you may be surprised
to find that Rash's big-screen debut as a

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:12.720
writer is significantly more serious than
his work as an actor.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.570
Rash collaborated with director Alexander
Payne on 2011's The Descendants, an acclaimed

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:22.840
Oscar-nominated family drama for which Rash
won the Oscar for Best Adapted Screenplay.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:26.990
His follow-up was the coming-of-age dramedy
The Way Way Back, which was met with similarly

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:27.820
wide acclaim.

